            <ArticleTitle>Regional cerebral blood flow (rCBF) in man during perception of radiant warmth and heat pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>353-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The present study concerns the effects of experimental pain (radiant warmth and heat pain) on regional cerebral blood flow (rCBF) in pretrained subjects. The radiant warmth caused a general rCBF increase. However, if anxiety was avoided, heat pain caused the general rCBF level to return towards the level at rest. Thus, pain sensation per se may not cause a larger rCBF (and metabolic) response than that of the localized tactile stimulation, provided that the element of psychic apprehension and anxiety is eliminated or controlled.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
